//! storage/lwlocklist.h - the predefined (individual) LWLock list (X-macro database).
//!
//! In C this header is a pure X-macro "list" file with deliberately no include
//! guard.  It is a sequence of `PG_LWLOCK(lockidx, lockname)` invocations; the
//! caller defines the `PG_LWLOCK` macro before `#include`-ing the file to
//! extract whatever projection it needs.  The canonical consumer is
//! `generate-lwlocknames.pl`, which turns each entry into a
//!     #define <lockname>Lock   (&MainLWLockArray[<lockidx>].lock)
//! line in the autogenerated `lwlocknames.h`, plus
//!     #define NUM_INDIVIDUAL_LWLOCKS  (<last lockidx> + 1)
//!
//! Rust has no textual-include / X-macro mechanism, so this module materializes
//! the projections the rest of the tree actually consumes:
//!   * a `<Name>_LWLOCK_ID` integer index constant for every defined lock (its
//!     value is the explicit lockidx from the C list, NOT a running counter -
//!     several positions are deliberately skipped because the locks that once
//!     occupied them were removed; gaps are left to avoid renumbering for the
//!     benefit of DTrace and other external debugging scripts),
//!   * `NUM_INDIVIDUAL_LWLOCKS`, the count used to size `MainLWLockArray`'s
//!     individual-lock region (last index + 1),
//!   * a `LWLOCK_NAMES` table of (index, name) pairs mirroring the projection
//!     `lwlocknames.h` / wait_event reporting consumes.
//!
//! The C names here intentionally omit the "Lock" suffix (to appease the C
//! preprocessor); the suffix is added when the `<Name>Lock` macro is generated.
//! We keep the bare identifiers verbatim in the index-constant names and store
//! the human-facing "<Name>Lock" string in the table.
//!
//! The actual `&MainLWLockArray[idx].lock` pointers cannot be formed here -
//! `MainLWLockArray` is a runtime global defined in lwlock.c, not in this
//! header.  When lwlock.{h,c} land, the `<Name>Lock` accessor macros should be
//! generated from the `*_LWLOCK_ID` constants below.

use std::ffi::c_int;

//
// Predefined LWLock indices (the lockidx argument of each PG_LWLOCK entry).
//
// IMPORTANT: these values are the *explicit* indices from lwlocklist.h, not a
// running counter.  Skipped indices (0, 10-12, 14-15, 26, 31, 38, 42, 45)
// correspond to locks that were removed; the gaps are preserved on purpose.
// New locks must be appended at the end so existing indices never shift.
//

